  (75749, 199, 'As a little girl, I loved playing sports.', 4.543, 7.420, NULL),
  (75750, 199, 'I also loved painting my fingernails', 8.004, 10.215, NULL),
  (75751, 199, 'and parading around my mother''s house
in her high-heeled shoes.', 10.257, 13.718, NULL),
  (75752, 199, 'From the beginning of time,
I have lived in contrasting worlds.', 14.427, 18.014, NULL),
  (75753, 199, 'Contrasting and often conflicting ideas', 18.431, 22.185, NULL),
  (75754, 199, 'about what women should and shouldn''t do,', 22.185, 24.479, NULL),
  (75755, 199, 'or maybe how we should and shouldn''t look.', 24.521, 27.023, NULL),
  (75756, 199, 'A pivotal moment came for me
when I was a sophomore in high school.', 27.941, 31.486, NULL),
  (75757, 199, 'My mother took me to an audition
for a modeling agency,', 31.528, 35.490, NULL),
  (75758, 199, 'and I can''t tell you
how nervous I was that day', 35.532, 37.742, NULL),
  (75759, 199, 'or even what I was wearing.', 37.742, 39.744, NULL),
  (75760, 199, 'But I will never forget
the way I felt the next morning.', 39.744, 42.872, NULL),
  (75761, 199, 'As I was walking into school,
I got a phone call.', 44.165, 47.002, NULL),
  (75762, 199, 'They wanted to sign me.', 47.627, 49.045, NULL),
  (75763, 199, 'As a plus-size model.', 50.046, 51.590, NULL),
  (75764, 199, 'I was a size 10, by the way.', 52.591, 54.426, NULL),
  (75765, 199, 'If I could go back and talk
to that 16-year-old version of myself,', 55.135, 59.347, NULL),
  (75766, 199, 'I would tell her exactly
what my own mother told me that day.', 59.347, 62.809, NULL),
  (75767, 199, '"You were built for bigger
and better things."', 63.143, 65.854, NULL),
  (75768, 199, 'So I turned down their offer,', 66.354, 67.939, NULL),
  (75769, 199, 'and I went on to play
NCAA college softball,', 67.939, 71.109, NULL),
  (75770, 199, 'where I was a weightlifter
of the year, twice.', 71.109, 74.904, NULL),
  (75771, 199, 'I loved being in the gym and I loved
throwing those heavy weights around,', 75.655, 79.868, NULL),
  (75772, 199, 'but what I really loved the most
was the confidence', 79.868, 82.662, NULL),
  (75773, 199, 'that my physical strength instilled in me.', 82.704, 85.123, NULL),
  (75774, 199, 'But there was always this nagging feeling,', 86.249, 89.336, NULL),
  (75775, 199, 'this sort of whisper
in the back of my mind,', 89.336, 92.881, NULL),
  (75776, 199, 'and it sounded a lot like
the voice on the phone that day.', 92.922, 95.800, NULL),
  (75777, 199, 'Telling me that I was different,', 96.676, 98.845, NULL),
  (75778, 199, 'somehow not ideal.', 98.845, 101.056, NULL),
  (75779, 199, 'Because I would shrink inside', 101.973, 104.184, NULL),
  (75780, 199, 'every time somebody commented
on my muscular body.', 104.184, 107.729, NULL),
  (75781, 199, 'You see, at the time,
the only social media we had', 109.105, 111.483, NULL),
  (75782, 199, 'was something called
“Cosmopolitan Magazine.”', 111.524, 113.985, NULL),
  (75783, 199, '(Laughter)', 113.985, 115.153, NULL),
  (75784, 199, 'And those Cosmo cover girls,', 115.612, 117.072, NULL),
  (75785, 199, 'well, they didn''t have a lot of muscles.', 117.113, 120.075, NULL),
  (75786, 199, 'Frankly, they looked kind of frail,', 120.075, 122.327, NULL),
  (75787, 199, 'somewhat unhealthy.', 122.369, 123.870, NULL),
  (75788, 199, 'But to the world,', 124.454, 125.914, NULL),
  (75789, 199, 'they were beautiful
and they were idolized.', 125.955, 128.917, NULL),
  (75790, 199, 'And as a young girl,
I let that frame the way that I looked', 129.250, 132.379, NULL),
  (75791, 199, 'and thought about myself.', 132.420, 134.005, NULL),
  (75792, 199, 'And this is the problem.', 134.673, 136.132, NULL),
  (75793, 199, 'Even though something like weightlifting', 136.883, 138.802, NULL),
  (75794, 199, 'has a multitude of proven health benefits,', 138.843, 141.888, NULL),
  (75795, 199, 'there continues to be a stigma for women', 141.888, 144.516, NULL),
  (75796, 199, 'based on cultural and societal myths', 144.557, 146.851, NULL),
  (75797, 199, 'about what women should and shouldn''t do.', 146.893, 149.229, NULL),
  (75798, 199, 'I believe that if we
can create a new culture', 149.979, 153.608, NULL),
  (75799, 199, 'that shifts the focus
from aesthetics to health,', 153.608, 157.612, NULL),
  (75800, 199, 'that women''s health', 157.612, 159.197, NULL),
  (75801, 199, 'and frankly, everybody''s health', 159.197, 160.949, NULL),
  (75802, 199, 'could be transformed forever.', 160.949, 162.909, NULL),
  (75803, 199, 'Now, I''d like to be able to tell you
that after college,', 163.993, 166.621, NULL),
  (75804, 199, 'I continued to train
really hard in the gym,', 166.663, 168.957, NULL),
  (75805, 199, 'ate a perfect diet
amidst a family and children,', 168.957, 171.418, NULL),
  (75806, 199, 'and a stressful career.', 171.418, 173.128, NULL),
  (75807, 199, 'But that is not what happened.', 173.962, 176.005, NULL),
  (75808, 199, 'What really happened
is I went to medical school,', 176.673, 180.093, NULL),
  (75809, 199, 'I survived residency,', 180.135, 182.220, NULL),
  (75810, 199, 'I got married and had three children,', 182.262, 184.472, NULL),
  (75811, 199, 'and I woke up one day', 184.514, 186.057, NULL),
  (75812, 199, 'dealing with the same metabolic diseases', 186.099, 188.435, NULL),
  (75813, 199, 'that I was helping my own patients
manage through medication.', 188.435, 191.730, NULL),
  (75814, 199, 'I could see it, I could feel it.', 192.355, 195.233, NULL),
  (75815, 199, 'But I told myself that I would just
take care of it when I had more time.', 195.275, 199.654, NULL),
  (75816, 199, 'And so many of us let ourselves believe', 200.280, 202.741, NULL),
  (75817, 199, 'that we have a lot of time.', 202.741, 204.534, NULL),
  (75818, 199, 'Now, according to the CDC,', 205.744, 207.537, NULL),
  (75819, 199, 'the top three killers of women
is heart disease,', 207.579, 211.249, NULL),
  (75820, 199, 'cancer and stroke.', 211.249, 213.042, NULL),
  (75821, 199, 'The major contributing factor
to these causes of death', 213.752, 216.796, NULL),
  (75822, 199, 'is what I call metabolic disease.', 216.838, 219.090, NULL),
  (75823, 199, 'And even though our modern,
high-fat, high-carbohydrate,', 219.883, 223.344, NULL),
  (75824, 199, 'highly processed diets play a major role,', 223.344, 227.557, NULL),
  (75825, 199, 'there''s one thing that women
are not doing across their lifespan', 227.557, 231.311, NULL),
  (75826, 199, 'that could tremendously reduce
their risk of death,', 231.311, 234.773, NULL),
  (75827, 199, 'and that is building muscle.', 234.773, 237.400, NULL),
  (75828, 199, 'The data is actually very clear
when it comes to resistance training.', 238.318, 242.155, NULL),
  (75829, 199, 'So why aren''t more women doing it?', 242.697, 244.866, NULL),
  (75830, 199, 'The answer lies in three primary myths', 246.326, 249.829, NULL),
  (75831, 199, 'that continue to exert a powerful force', 249.871, 252.624, NULL),
  (75832, 199, 'and prevent women from doing just that.', 252.665, 255.210, NULL),
  (75833, 199, 'Myth number one
is that if we lift weights,', 256.211, 259.172, NULL),
  (75834, 199, 'we''re going to get big and bulky.', 259.172, 261.591, NULL),
  (75835, 199, 'Women think that if they pick up
a 20-pound dumbbell,', 262.342, 264.886, NULL),
  (75836, 199, 'that they will somehow look
like Arnold Schwarzenegger.', 264.886, 267.514, NULL),
  (75837, 199, '(Laughter)', 267.514, 268.515, NULL),
  (75838, 199, 'Now, I can tell you that it takes
years of very serious training --', 268.515, 274.604, NULL),
  (75839, 199, 'and steroids --', 274.729, 276.356, NULL),
  (75840, 199, 'to accumulate that kind of look.', 276.356, 278.817, NULL),
  (75841, 199, 'And if you take a look around the gym,', 279.317, 281.152, NULL),
  (75842, 199, 'you can see that achieving the physique
of somebody like the Hulk', 281.194, 284.280, NULL),
  (75843, 199, 'is actually difficult, even for most men.', 284.280, 286.908, NULL),
  (75844, 199, 'Now we''re all born
with a certain genetic potential', 288.034, 290.745, NULL),
  (75845, 199, 'when it comes to our muscle
size and distribution.', 290.745, 293.706, NULL),
  (75846, 199, 'But beyond that, it takes
years of very serious training', 293.748, 296.960, NULL),
  (75847, 199, 'and a concerted effort to build
and maintain that lean tissue.', 297.001, 300.922, NULL),
  (75848, 199, 'Although women have a similar relative
natural muscular potential to men,', 301.840, 307.345, NULL),
  (75849, 199, 'we''re also prone to the same
age-related muscle loss.', 307.345, 311.349, NULL),
  (75850, 199, 'And the medical term for that
is called sarcopenia.', 311.349, 314.435, NULL),
  (75851, 199, 'On average, after age 40,', 315.353, 317.981, NULL),
  (75852, 199, 'we lose about one percent
of our muscle size.', 317.981, 321.317, NULL),
  (75853, 199, 'With these age-related declines,', 321.317, 323.194, NULL),
  (75854, 199, 'we become weak, we become frail,', 323.236, 325.822, NULL),
  (75855, 199, 'and the risk of all
chronic diseases starts to go up.', 325.864, 329.534, NULL),
  (75856, 199, 'In a study, they found
the more days, time and effort', 330.326, 332.829, NULL),
  (75857, 199, 'that women devote to strength training,', 332.871, 334.789, NULL),
  (75858, 199, 'the better their body composition is.', 334.831, 336.916, NULL),
  (75859, 199, 'Myth number two
is that weightlifting is hard', 337.876, 341.337, NULL),
  (75860, 199, 'or maybe too hard on our bodies.', 341.379, 344.382, NULL),
  (75861, 199, 'You see, even as women,
if we make a decision to go work out,', 344.382, 347.844, NULL),
  (75862, 199, 'we''re more likely to choose
the cardio equipment.', 347.886, 350.388, NULL),
  (75863, 199, 'But let me dispel this myth.', 350.847, 352.682, NULL),
  (75864, 199, 'Strength training meets you
where you''re at.', 352.724, 355.435, NULL),
  (75865, 199, 'The first day you''re in the gym,', 355.810, 357.353, NULL),
  (75866, 199, 'you''re not going to be doing
a 500-pound back squat.', 357.353, 360.398, NULL),
  (75867, 199, 'It''s about being stronger today
than you were yesterday.', 360.940, 364.444, NULL),
  (75868, 199, 'But we don''t have the same role models
when we walk into the gym,', 365.403, 369.198, NULL),
  (75869, 199, 'because when we walk in', 369.198, 370.825, NULL),
  (75870, 199, 'and we see this man
with multiple plates on the bar', 370.867, 375.079, NULL),
  (75871, 199, 'and he''s making crazy faces --', 375.079, 377.081, NULL),
  (75872, 199, '(Laughter)', 377.081, 378.291, NULL),
  (75873, 199, 'And even crazier sounds -- Arrgh!', 378.333, 381.544, NULL),
  (75874, 199, 'It''s not very inviting to most women.', 381.544, 384.714, NULL),
  (75875, 199, 'But you see, women actually have
an advantage over men', 385.131, 388.593, NULL),
  (75876, 199, 'in certain parts of our life.', 388.593, 390.803, NULL),
  (75877, 199, 'We make more estrogen than men,', 390.803, 393.348, NULL),
  (75878, 199, 'and estrogen means
that we don''t fatigue as fast', 393.348, 396.225, NULL),
  (75879, 199, 'and we actually recover faster.', 396.267, 398.353, NULL),
  (75880, 199, 'Which means that we can handle
more volume in weight training', 398.770, 402.732, NULL),
  (75881, 199, 'without overtraining.', 402.732, 404.442, NULL),
  (75882, 199, 'Myth number three', 406.027, 407.320, NULL),
  (75883, 199, 'is that weightlifting is for the boys', 407.320, 409.447, NULL),
  (75884, 199, 'and aerobics is for the girls.', 409.489, 411.532, NULL),
  (75885, 199, 'You''ve seen it when you walk into the gym,', 412.325, 414.410, NULL),
  (75886, 199, 'this big room of treadmills, ellipticals
and stair climbers filled with women.', 414.452, 418.790, NULL),
  (75887, 199, 'But in the “Journal of Exercise Science,”', 419.749, 421.751, NULL),
  (75888, 199, 'they found for every woman', 421.793, 423.211, NULL),
  (75889, 199, 'that was using the free-weight
section of the gym,', 423.252, 426.422, NULL),
  (75890, 199, 'there was 27 men.', 426.464, 429.008, NULL),
  (75891, 199, 'This disparity continues
to make it difficult to foster health', 430.051, 433.930, NULL),
  (75892, 199, 'for women across their lifespan.', 433.972, 436.099, NULL),
  (75893, 199, 'Resistance training happens to be
the only non-pharmacological intervention', 437.016, 442.146, NULL),
  (75894, 199, 'that has been consistently shown', 442.188, 444.399, NULL),
  (75895, 199, 'to offset these age-related declines', 444.440, 447.276, NULL),
  (75896, 199, 'in skeletal muscle mass,
strength and power.', 447.276, 450.113, NULL),
  (75897, 199, 'This cannot be achieved on a treadmill.', 450.697, 453.491, NULL),
  (75898, 199, 'The male bias in gym culture
is literally everywhere,', 454.534, 458.162, NULL),
  (75899, 199, 'and it''s time for us to counter it.', 458.204, 460.331, NULL),
  (75900, 199, 'All humans have muscles.', 461.040, 462.792, NULL),
  (75901, 199, 'Muscles make us healthy.', 463.167, 464.877, NULL),
  (75902, 199, 'Muscles combat the top three
killers of women.', 465.378, 468.589, NULL),
  (75903, 199, 'Resistance training
meets you where you''re at.', 469.924, 472.552, NULL),
  (75904, 199, 'We think that we need these fancy machines', 473.761, 476.097, NULL),
  (75905, 199, 'and free weights to build muscle.', 476.097, 477.932, NULL),
  (75906, 199, 'But in a 2021 study,', 478.391, 480.435, NULL),
  (75907, 199, 'they examined a population
of women 65 and older.', 480.476, 483.646, NULL),
  (75908, 199, 'They put these women through body weight', 484.188, 486.357, NULL),
  (75909, 199, 'and resistance band training', 486.399, 488.443, NULL),
  (75910, 199, 'three times per week for 16 weeks.', 488.443, 491.154, NULL),
  (75911, 199, 'And do you know what they found?', 491.154, 493.114, NULL),
  (75912, 199, 'In the treatment group,', 493.156, 494.449, NULL),
  (75913, 199, 'these women improved their strength,', 494.490, 496.743, NULL),
  (75914, 199, 'improved their functional fitness,', 496.784, 498.828, NULL),
  (75915, 199, 'improved their grip strength', 498.828, 500.580, NULL),
  (75916, 199, 'and even improved their gait speed.', 500.621, 502.957, NULL),
  (75917, 199, 'The amount of work required
to see benefit is actually quite small.', 503.499, 508.588, NULL),
  (75918, 199, 'You can lift weights,', 509.213, 510.673, NULL),
  (75919, 199, 'you can lift your groceries,', 510.715, 512.425, NULL),
  (75920, 199, 'you can lift your children,', 512.425, 514.218, NULL),
  (75921, 199, 'or when you''re first starting,', 514.218, 515.678, NULL),
  (75922, 199, 'you can even just lift
your own body weight.', 515.678, 518.389, NULL),
  (75923, 199, 'It''s my mission to make sure
that women live a long and healthy life,', 519.599, 524.729, NULL),
  (75924, 199, 'and that means building
and maintaining their muscle.', 524.729, 528.149, NULL),
  (75925, 199, 'What made me realize the urgency
of my own health problems', 529.484, 532.987, NULL),
  (75926, 199, 'is when I lost one
of my best friends very suddenly,', 533.029, 536.699, NULL),
  (75927, 199, 'at the age of 29.', 536.699, 538.117, NULL),
  (75928, 199, 'And there I stood,', 539.410, 540.995, NULL),
  (75929, 199, 'face-to-face with my own mortality.', 541.037, 544.123, NULL),
  (75930, 199, 'I was weak.', 544.999, 546.167, NULL),
  (75931, 199, 'I was tired.', 546.167, 547.710, NULL),
  (75932, 199, 'What was missing?', 547.752, 549.087, NULL),
  (75933, 199, 'Something that I had literally
wished away after college.', 550.088, 553.925, NULL),
  (75934, 199, 'Muscle.', 554.801, 556.010, NULL),
  (75935, 199, 'I knew I had to get back into the gym
and start training again,', 556.803, 561.641, NULL),
  (75936, 199, 'but I had to get over the fact
that through my formative years,', 561.641, 564.769, NULL),
  (75937, 199, 'society told me
that muscles were for boys.', 564.811, 567.814, NULL),
  (75938, 199, 'Two years after I set out
to regain my health,', 569.941, 572.527, NULL),
  (75939, 199, 'I competed on a show called
“Titan Games” with real, everyday heroes.', 572.527, 576.322, NULL),
  (75940, 199, 'And in that same year,
I competed at the Mrs. America pageant.', 576.364, 579.742, NULL),
  (75941, 199, 'Doing both of these things
in the same year', 580.576, 583.037, NULL),
  (75942, 199, 'was my attempt to shatter
the cultural stereotypes', 583.079, 586.541, NULL),
  (75943, 199, 'that continue to tell us
that muscles are just for men.', 586.582, 590.336, NULL),
  (75944, 199, 'So let me leave you
with a very clear call to action.', 591.462, 594.465, NULL),
  (75945, 199, 'Start lifting heavy things now.', 595.424, 598.386, NULL),
  (75946, 199, 'Nobody can do it for you.', 599.095, 600.805, NULL),
  (75947, 199, 'Literally.', 601.389, 602.557, NULL),
  (75948, 199, 'Your older self will thank you.', 603.057, 604.934, NULL),
  (75949, 199, 'Or if you are your older self,
it''s never too late.', 605.226, 608.646, NULL),
  (75950, 199, 'This is not about aesthetics.', 609.397, 611.149, NULL),
  (75951, 199, 'This is about health.', 611.190, 612.400, NULL),
  (75952, 199, 'And physically strong women
are healthy women.', 612.817, 616.195, NULL),
  (75953, 199, 'Thank you.', 616.821, 617.989, NULL),
  (75954, 199, '(Applause)', 617.989, 622.368, NULL),
  (75955, 200, 'On a usual Saturday,
you wouldn''t find me here.', 5.167, 9.375, NULL),
  (75956, 200, 'And I mean, of course, I don’t give
TEDx Talks every Saturday.', 11.208, 15.417, NULL),
  (75957, 200, 'But what I mean is, you probably
wouldn''t find me in a museum.', 16.167, 20.208, NULL),
  (75958, 200, 'Rather, if you were looking for me,', 20.667, 22.417, NULL),
  (75959, 200, 'you''d probably have to go
to the mountains,', 22.417, 25.667, NULL),
  (75960, 200, 'and that is because
I absolutely love hiking.', 25.708, 28.875, NULL),
  (75961, 200, 'And in addition to just being outside
and being in nature,', 28.917, 33.125, NULL),
  (75962, 200, 'what I really like is this feeling
of physical exhaustion,', 33.125, 36.625, NULL),
  (75963, 200, 'sort of this satisfaction
you can feel in every single muscle.', 36.666, 40.791, NULL),
  (75964, 200, 'And in the evening,
when it''s time to go to bed,', 42.291, 46.708, NULL),
  (75965, 200, 'I absolutely cherish
this amazing sleepiness', 46.750, 50.333, NULL),
  (75966, 200, 'that fills you from head to toe.', 50.375, 52.166, NULL),
  (75967, 200, 'Now you might say, "Well, of course
she''s tired. She''s been hiking all day.', 54.000, 58.708, NULL),
  (75968, 200, 'Plus, not to forget about the effects
of supposedly fresh air."', 59.416, 63.916, NULL),
  (75969, 200, 'But there is one factor
that most likely outweighs', 65.625, 69.000, NULL),
  (75970, 200, 'the effects of fresh air
and perhaps even physical activity,', 69.042, 74.917, NULL),
  (75971, 200, 'and that is exposure to natural daylight.', 74.958, 78.458, NULL),
  (75972, 200, 'And today, I hope to convince you
that we should all appreciate daylight', 79.542, 83.583, NULL),
  (75973, 200, 'as sort of a natural soporific.', 83.625, 86.583, NULL),
  (75974, 200, 'And hopefully, by the end of the talk,', 87.167, 89.708, NULL),
  (75975, 200, 'you will also recognize
that we should all pay more attention', 89.750, 93.792, NULL),
  (75976, 200, 'to our daily light diet.', 93.833, 96.208, NULL),
  (75977, 200, 'But let''s start with the question
why daylight is so important', 96.792, 100.708, NULL),
  (75978, 200, 'for our organism, for health
and, eventually, for sleep.', 100.750, 105.208, NULL),
  (75979, 200, 'Now throughout your body,
in each and every single cell,', 106.500, 110.208, NULL),
  (75980, 200, 'there is a tiny molecular clock
ticking inside it.', 110.250, 113.958, NULL),
  (75981, 200, 'And to keep these millions
of clocks in sync,', 114.667, 118.208, NULL),
  (75982, 200, 'we have one central body clock
that is located in the brain,', 118.250, 122.500, NULL),
  (75983, 200, 'in an area which we call the hypothalamus.', 122.500, 125.250, NULL),
  (75984, 200, 'And like the conductor of an orchestra,', 126.333, 128.708, NULL),
  (75985, 200, 'it communicates the time of day
to each of these molecular clocks.', 128.750, 134.625, NULL),
  (75986, 200, 'And this way, it is able
to regulate bodily processes', 134.625, 138.083, NULL),
  (75987, 200, 'in sync with the time of day.', 138.125, 140.458, NULL),
  (75988, 200, 'For example,', 141.542, 142.708, NULL),
  (75989, 200, 'it causes your body temperature
to increase in the morning', 142.750, 146.000, NULL),
  (75990, 200, 'and to decrease in the evening.', 146.042, 147.667, NULL),
  (75991, 200, 'It choreographs the release
of certain hormones at appropriate times.', 148.333, 153.083, NULL),
  (75992, 200, 'And in the evening,
when it’s time to go to bed,', 153.125, 156.625, NULL),
  (75993, 200, 'it will also make you tired and sleepy.', 156.667, 159.458, NULL),
  (75994, 200, 'But given this precise synchronization
between internal or biological time', 160.958, 166.917, NULL),
  (75995, 200, 'and external time or environmental time,', 166.958, 171.208, NULL),
  (75996, 200, 'it seems clear that the body clock', 171.250, 173.750, NULL),
  (75997, 200, 'cannot be blind or isolated
or shut off from the environment,', 173.750, 178.958, NULL),
  (75998, 200, 'but rather, it needs to receive
information about the time of day', 179.000, 183.250, NULL),
  (75999, 200, 'from the environment', 183.292, 184.833, NULL),
  (76000, 200, 'for it to synchronize with sun time.', 184.875, 188.250, NULL),
  (76001, 200, 'And this is achieved by close connections', 189.667, 192.125, NULL),
  (76002, 200, 'between the internal biological clock
in the brain and our eyes.', 192.167, 196.917, NULL),
  (76003, 200, 'And now you may know
that in the human retina,', 197.958, 200.875, NULL),
  (76004, 200, 'there are different types of receptors,', 200.875, 203.542, NULL),
  (76005, 200, 'so photoreceptors,
so receptors that sense light.', 203.542, 207.583, NULL),
  (76006, 200, 'And classically, we distinguish two types,
the rods and the cones.', 208.542, 214.000, NULL),
  (76007, 200, 'Now the rods only contribute
to a visual impression', 214.958, 218.208, NULL),
  (76008, 200, 'under very dim lighting conditions.', 218.250, 221.250, NULL),
  (76009, 200, 'And here, in the background,
you now see a picture', 221.958, 224.958, NULL),
  (76010, 200, 'that might, more or less, be brought about
by only the involvement of the rods.', 225.000, 230.250, NULL),
  (76011, 200, 'And what you can see
is that it''s only shades of gray,', 231.083, 234.667, NULL),
  (76012, 200, 'it''s slightly blurred,', 234.708, 236.417, NULL),
  (76013, 200, 'and around the point of fixation,
which is indicated by the gray dot here,', 236.458, 241.750, NULL),
  (76014, 200, 'you have a little scotoma,', 241.792, 243.250, NULL),
  (76015, 200, 'so an area where you can''t
really see anything.', 243.292, 246.000, NULL),
  (76016, 200, 'Now who recognizes what that is?', 246.000, 248.167, NULL),
  (76017, 200, 'Yeah, excellent.', 248.958, 250.167, NULL),
  (76018, 200, 'But it''s going to be way easier,', 250.208, 252.083, NULL),
  (76019, 200, 'and the majority of you
will recognize what it is,', 252.125, 254.958, NULL),
  (76020, 200, 'when I now switch to the next slide,', 254.958, 256.833, NULL),
  (76021, 200, 'which is brought about
by the involvement of the cones.', 256.875, 260.500, NULL),
  (76022, 200, 'Of course, it''s the town hall of Basel,
but now you can appreciate the colors,', 260.917, 267.583, NULL),
  (76023, 200, 'fine details.', 267.625, 268.958, NULL),
  (76024, 200, 'And if this was animated,
you''d even see fine details in motion.', 269.000, 274.167, NULL),
  (76025, 200, 'But this is not the whole story,
because only fairly recently,', 274.167, 278.500, NULL),
  (76026, 200, 'only in the early 2000s,
another type of cell has been discovered,', 278.542, 283.625, NULL),
  (76027, 200, 'and we call them retinal ganglion cells.', 283.667, 286.250, NULL),
  (76028, 200, 'Now, you might ask yourselves,
"What picture is she going to show next?"', 287.708, 291.833, NULL),
  (76029, 200, 'But I''ll have to disappoint you ...', 293.833, 295.875, NULL),
  (76030, 200, 'I’m not, and I actually can’t, 
show another picture.', 297.000, 300.792, NULL),
  (76031, 200, 'Because from all we know,', 300.792, 302.458, NULL),
  (76032, 200, 'these cells do not contribute
to a visual impression.', 302.500, 306.625, NULL),
  (76033, 200, 'But they are exclusively designed', 307.750, 310.125, NULL),
  (76034, 200, 'to sense short wavelength
proportions in daylight.', 310.125, 314.583, NULL),
  (76035, 200, 'Sometimes, we also call this blue light.', 315.542, 318.542, NULL),
  (76036, 200, 'So they''re designed to extract important
information about the time of day', 319.375, 323.750, NULL),
  (76037, 200, 'from the environment', 323.750, 325.083, NULL),
  (76038, 200, 'and pass this on
to the internal biological clock', 325.125, 328.417, NULL),
  (76039, 200, 'in the brain.', 328.458, 329.917, NULL),
  (76040, 200, 'And I guess you''ve all experienced
how well this biological timing system,', 329.958, 335.250, NULL),
  (76041, 200, 'this connection between our biological
clock and the external world, or our eyes,', 335.292, 341.708, NULL),
  (76042, 200, 'works when we, for example,
travel across time zones.', 341.708, 345.708, NULL),
  (76043, 200, 'Now how much light do we actually need?', 346.875, 350.417, NULL),
  (76044, 200, 'How much light is enough
for the positive effects on, for instance,', 350.458, 354.958, NULL),
  (76045, 200, 'sleep to occur?', 355.000, 356.542, NULL),
  (76046, 200, 'And I have to admit,
this is not so easy to answer.', 357.458, 361.000, NULL),
  (76047, 200, 'But I think what we have
to keep in keep in mind', 361.042, 364.042, NULL),
  (76048, 200, 'is that the biological timing system
has evolved under the open sky', 364.083, 368.875, NULL),
  (76049, 200, 'and not in offices or museums.', 368.917, 371.917, NULL),
  (76050, 200, 'So it is also optimally tuned
to the conditions we find outside.', 373.167, 378.750, NULL),
  (76051, 200, 'Office light or light in rooms', 379.125, 381.375, NULL),
  (76052, 200, 'is in no way comparable
to what we find outside.', 381.417, 385.375, NULL),
  (76053, 200, 'And therefore,
from a scientific perspective,', 386.500, 389.042, NULL),
  (76054, 200, 'I can only recommend you to spend
as much time as you can', 389.083, 392.917, NULL),
  (76055, 200, 'under the open sky,', 392.958, 394.792, NULL),
  (76056, 200, 'but try to make it at least
30 minutes per day.', 394.792, 397.792, NULL),
  (76057, 200, 'Now let''s finally talk about sleep.', 399.792, 402.292, NULL),
  (76058, 200, 'And I''ve repeatedly alluded
to the fact that daylight is beneficial', 402.333, 407.000, NULL),
  (76059, 200, 'and important for sleep.', 407.042, 409.167, NULL),
  (76060, 200, 'And in this context,
I''d like to share a little story.', 410.292, 413.292, NULL),
  (76061, 200, 'So last year, I had to go
to hospital for surgery.', 414.375, 418.083, NULL),
  (76062, 200, 'And generally, the hospital environment
is a very challenging one for sleep.', 419.583, 424.250, NULL),
  (76063, 200, 'Because you spend way too much time
in bed, not moving much.', 426.292, 430.208, NULL),
  (76064, 200, 'You might be in pain.', 430.250, 432.375, NULL),
  (76065, 200, 'Now and then, someone comes in
to check on you, even during the night.', 432.375, 436.625, NULL),
  (76066, 200, 'And if you''re as lucky as I was,
you have a snoring roommate.', 436.625, 440.542, NULL),
  (76067, 200, '(Laughter)', 440.583, 442.083, NULL),
  (76068, 200, 'And because you, of course,
don''t get a lot of daylight.', 443.042, 447.625, NULL),
  (76069, 200, 'And that''s why many people,
while they''re in hospital,', 448.583, 451.292, NULL),
  (76070, 200, 'ask for sleep medication.', 451.333, 453.583, NULL),
  (76071, 200, 'But as I consider myself
to be a generally good sleeper,', 453.625, 457.708, NULL),
  (76072, 200, 'and also because I know
how these drugs work,', 457.750, 461.000, NULL),
  (76073, 200, 'I didn’t want this.', 461.042, 463.500, NULL),
  (76074, 200, 'But I knew there was something I could do
to help my body sleep as well as possible,', 463.500, 469.417, NULL),
  (76075, 200, 'even in this situation.', 469.458, 471.250, NULL),
  (76076, 200, 'And that was to maximize
exposure to daylight.', 472.250, 477.375, NULL),
  (76077, 200, 'Because as a sleep scientist,', 477.375, 480.000, NULL),
  (76078, 200, 'of course, I know
about the research findings.', 480.042, 482.833, NULL),
  (76079, 200, 'I know that the more 
daylight that you get,', 482.875, 486.208, NULL),
  (76080, 200, 'the more tired you will be in the evening,', 486.250, 489.458, NULL),
  (76081, 200, 'the easier it will be
for you to fall asleep', 489.500, 492.375, NULL),
  (76082, 200, 'and the better the experienced
sleep quality.', 492.417, 495.583, NULL),
  (76083, 200, 'And in addition,', 495.583, 496.750, NULL),
  (76084, 200, 'daylight exposure has also been shown
to increase proportions of deep sleep,', 496.792, 501.208, NULL),
  (76085, 200, 'which again has been linked
to processes of tissue repair,', 501.250, 506.000, NULL),
  (76086, 200, 'something not to be
underestimated after surgery.', 506.042, 509.958, NULL),
  (76087, 200, 'And beyond the effects on sleep,', 510.792, 512.333, NULL),
  (76088, 200, 'we know that patients in brighter rooms
experience less pain and less stress.', 512.375, 518.166, NULL),
  (76089, 200, 'And also the effects of daylight
on mood are well established.', 518.208, 522.916, NULL),
  (76090, 200, 'So I thought it might be time for me
to put into practice what I often preach.', 524.041, 528.875, NULL),
  (76091, 200, 'And I have to confess that usually', 528.875, 530.583, NULL),
  (76092, 200, 'I''m much better at giving advice
than following it myself.', 530.625, 534.333, NULL),
  (76093, 200, 'But here''s what I did.', 535.333, 536.916, NULL),
  (76094, 200, 'So first, when it became free,', 537.750, 540.333, NULL),
  (76095, 200, 'I asked the nurses to move
my bed to the window place,', 540.333, 543.916, NULL),
  (76096, 200, 'because that does not only give you
power of the fresh air supply,', 543.958, 547.208, NULL),
  (76097, 200, 'which is highly recommended
in the hospital,', 547.250, 550.875, NULL),
  (76098, 200, 'but it also maximizes daylight exposure
even though you''re inside.', 550.875, 555.125, NULL),
  (76099, 200, 'Second, as soon as I could
leave my bed again,', 555.916, 558.583, NULL),
  (76100, 200, 'I went to the park for short walks.', 558.625, 561.083, NULL),
  (76101, 200, 'And last, every meal I had,
I tried to take on the little balcony.', 561.125, 566.375, NULL),
  (76102, 200, 'Did it work?', 567.791, 568.958, NULL),
  (76103, 200, 'Well, it''s a bit difficult to say,', 570.291, 572.500, NULL),
  (76104, 200, 'because it of course
was a single-case study.', 572.541, 576.125, NULL),
  (76105, 200, 'But from patients suffering from insomnia,', 576.166, 578.458, NULL),
  (76106, 200, 'so one of the most common sleep disorders,', 578.500, 581.958, NULL),
  (76107, 200, 'we know that light therapy is beneficial.', 582.000, 585.291, NULL),
  (76108, 200, 'And therefore, I would like
to encourage all of you,', 586.375, 588.916, NULL),
  (76109, 200, 'and perhaps especially those
who might belong', 588.958, 592.250, NULL),
  (76110, 200, 'to the 25 percent
suffering from sleep problems,', 592.250, 595.958, NULL),
  (76111, 200, 'to start your own single-case study.', 596.000, 598.916, NULL),
  (76112, 200, 'Thank you.', 599.875, 601.000, NULL),
  (76113, 200, '(Applause)', 601.041, 602.500, NULL),
  (76114, 201, 'So I''m going to ask you
to participate in an experiment,', 5.140, 10.140, NULL),
  (76115, 201, 'which is that when you leave this room,', 10.180, 12.580, NULL),
  (76116, 201, 'when you go out into the world,', 12.580, 14.060, NULL),
  (76117, 201, 'today, tomorrow,
whenever you feel like it,', 14.100, 16.100, NULL),
  (76118, 201, 'I''d like you to ask
and answer one question', 16.140, 20.660, NULL),
  (76119, 201, 'of someone who''s a stranger.', 20.700, 22.060, NULL),
  (76120, 201, 'You might meet them on the bus', 22.060, 23.580, NULL),
  (76121, 201, 'or walking down the street.', 23.580, 25.220, NULL),
  (76122, 201, 'And I''m going to show you the question', 25.260, 27.100, NULL),
  (76123, 201, 'that I''m going to ask you
to ask and answer.', 27.140, 29.220, NULL),
  (76124, 201, 'The question is:', 29.260, 30.420, NULL),
  (76125, 201, 'When was the last time you cried
in front of someone?', 30.420, 33.380, NULL),
  (76126, 201, 'Now just out of curiosity,', 34.340, 35.660, NULL),
  (76127, 201, 'how many of you are really
excited about this experiment?', 35.660, 38.940, NULL),
  (76128, 201, 'No hands went up whatsoever.', 38.980, 41.860, NULL),
  (76129, 201, 'And that makes sense, right?', 41.860, 43.260, NULL),
  (76130, 201, 'Because, like, there can be nothing
that seems more intimidating', 43.260, 46.340, NULL),
  (76131, 201, 'or less fun than finding a stranger,', 46.380, 48.820, NULL),
  (76132, 201, 'asking them when they''ve cried
in front of someone else,', 48.860, 51.540, NULL),
  (76133, 201, 'and then telling them about the time
you cried in front of someone else.', 51.540, 54.940, NULL),
  (76134, 201, 'But I''m going to try and convince you
over the next few minutes', 54.980, 57.940, NULL),
  (76135, 201, 'that this experiment
is not only worth doing,', 57.980, 60.100, NULL),
  (76136, 201, 'it’s worth doing whenever you can.', 60.100, 61.940, NULL),
  (76137, 201, 'Because it will make your life better.', 61.980, 64.340, NULL),
  (76138, 201, 'And to explain how I got to this,', 64.380, 65.980, NULL),
  (76139, 201, 'I have to tell you a little bit
of a story about me and my wife.', 66.020, 69.140, NULL),
  (76140, 201, 'A few years ago,
we got into this bad pattern.', 69.180, 71.380, NULL),
  (76141, 201, 'We''ve been married for 20 years,', 71.420, 73.020, NULL),
  (76142, 201, 'but I would come home
from a long day at the office.', 73.020, 75.460, NULL),
  (76143, 201, 'I was a reporter at "The New York
Times" at that point,', 75.500, 78.100, NULL),
  (76144, 201, 'and I would start
complaining about my day,', 78.100, 80.140, NULL),
  (76145, 201, 'about how I''m not appreciated enough.', 80.140, 81.940, NULL),
  (76146, 201, 'And my wife, very reasonably,
would offer me some great advice.', 81.940, 84.900, NULL),
  (76147, 201, 'She would say something like,', 84.940, 86.340, NULL),
  (76148, 201, '“Why don’t you take
your boss out to lunch,', 86.380, 88.420, NULL),
  (76149, 201, 'and you guys can get to know
each other a little bit better?"', 88.460, 91.340, NULL),
  (76150, 201, 'And instead of being able to hear her,
I would get even more upset.', 91.380, 94.540, NULL),
  (76151, 201, 'I would say things like,
"Why aren''t you supporting me?', 94.540, 97.140, NULL),
  (76152, 201, 'You should be outraged on my behalf."', 97.140, 98.940, NULL),
  (76153, 201, 'And she would get upset because I was
attacking her for giving me good advice.', 98.940, 102.620, NULL),
  (76154, 201, 'Anyone ever had an experience like this?', 102.660, 104.580, NULL),
  (76155, 201, 'It was not a good situation.', 104.620, 106.300, NULL),
  (76156, 201, 'And so I started talking to researchers', 106.340, 108.660, NULL),
  (76157, 201, 'who were studying communication.', 108.660, 110.260, NULL),
  (76158, 201, 'I asked them, why am I getting
into this pattern?', 110.260, 112.620, NULL),
  (76159, 201, 'And they said, "You''re making a mistake."', 112.660, 114.620, NULL),
  (76160, 201, 'We''re living through this golden age
of understanding communication,', 114.620, 117.820, NULL),
  (76161, 201, 'really for the first time', 117.860, 119.060, NULL),
  (76162, 201, 'because of advances
in neural imaging and data collection.', 119.060, 121.820, NULL),
  (76163, 201, 'And they said, one of the big things
that we''ve learned', 121.860, 124.460, NULL),
  (76164, 201, 'is that we tend to think of a discussion
as being just one conversation, right?', 124.460, 128.220, NULL),
  (76165, 201, 'We''re talking about one thing, my day,', 128.220, 130.060, NULL),
  (76166, 201, 'or the kids'' grades,
what to have for dinner.', 130.060, 132.180, NULL),
  (76167, 201, 'But what they said is that actually,', 132.220, 133.940, NULL),
  (76168, 201, 'each discussion contains
many different conversations.', 133.980, 138.300, NULL),
  (76169, 201, 'And in general, these conversations tend
to fall into one of three buckets.', 138.860, 142.460, NULL),
  (76170, 201, 'There''s these practical conversations,', 142.500, 144.740, NULL),
  (76171, 201, 'where we''re talking
about what''s this all about.', 144.740, 147.020, NULL),
  (76172, 201, 'What are we really discussing?', 147.020, 148.700, NULL),
  (76173, 201, 'But then there''s emotional conversations', 148.740, 150.660, NULL),
  (76174, 201, 'where we''re talking about how do we feel.', 150.700, 152.700, NULL),
  (76175, 201, 'And my goal is to share
with you my feelings,', 152.700, 154.860, NULL),
  (76176, 201, 'and I don''t want you to solve them.', 154.900, 156.660, NULL),
  (76177, 201, 'I want you to empathize.', 156.700, 158.700, NULL),
  (76178, 201, 'And then there''s conversations
that are social conversations', 158.740, 161.580, NULL),
  (76179, 201, 'about who we are,', 161.580, 162.780, NULL),
  (76180, 201, 'the social identities
that are important to us,', 162.780, 165.020, NULL),
  (76181, 201, 'how we relate to each other
and to society.', 165.020, 167.020, NULL),
  (76182, 201, 'And what the researchers said
is what we''ve learned', 167.060, 169.460, NULL),
  (76183, 201, 'is that if people are having different
conversations at the same moment,', 169.500, 173.300, NULL),
  (76184, 201, 'they can''t really hear each other,
they can''t really connect.', 173.300, 177.340, NULL),
  (76185, 201, 'And in fact, this is exactly
what was happening with me and my wife.', 177.340, 180.540, NULL),
  (76186, 201, 'I was coming home and having
an emotional conversation,', 180.580, 183.180, NULL),
  (76187, 201, 'my wife was responding
with a practical conversation.', 183.180, 185.700, NULL),
  (76188, 201, 'They are both legitimate conversations.', 185.700, 187.580, NULL),
  (76189, 201, 'But because we weren’t having
the same conversation', 187.620, 190.020, NULL),
  (76190, 201, 'at the same moment,', 190.060, 191.540, NULL),
  (76191, 201, 'we weren''t really communicating
with each other.', 191.580, 194.300, NULL),
  (76192, 201, 'And within neurology and psychology,', 194.700, 196.460, NULL),
  (76193, 201, 'this insight is known
as the matching principle,', 196.500, 198.780, NULL),
  (76194, 201, 'which says that successful
communication requires recognizing', 198.780, 201.860, NULL),
  (76195, 201, 'what kind of conversation is occurring
and then matching each other.', 201.900, 205.300, NULL),
  (76196, 201, 'But how do we do that?', 205.700, 207.100, NULL),
  (76197, 201, 'Well in schools, they’ve actually
taught teachers to do this.', 207.420, 210.540, NULL),
  (76198, 201, 'If you are a school teacher,', 210.580, 212.380, NULL),
  (76199, 201, 'you''ll probably learn at some point', 212.420, 214.340, NULL),
  (76200, 201, 'that if a student
comes up to you with a problem', 214.340, 216.660, NULL),
  (76201, 201, 'or they want something to talk about,', 216.660, 218.500, NULL),
  (76202, 201, 'you should ask them,
"Do you want to be helped,"', 218.540, 220.820, NULL),
  (76203, 201, 'which is a practical conversation.', 220.820, 222.460, NULL),
  (76204, 201, '"Do you want to be hugged,"
which is an emotional conversation.', 222.460, 225.420, NULL),
  (76205, 201, 'Or "Do you want to be heard,"
which is a social conversation.', 225.460, 228.340, NULL),
  (76206, 201, 'And it seems to work.', 228.340, 229.500, NULL),
  (76207, 201, 'It seems like if you ask students
what they need, they''ll tell you.', 229.540, 233.580, NULL),
  (76208, 201, 'But of course that is hard to do
in real life, right?', 233.620, 236.180, NULL),
  (76209, 201, 'If you go up and you ask someone
at work if they want a hug,', 236.220, 239.100, NULL),
  (76210, 201, 'HR might get involved,
so you might not want to do that.', 239.100, 242.260, NULL),
  (76211, 201, 'But luckily, there is another way
of doing this for all of us normal people,', 242.820, 247.260, NULL),
  (76212, 201, 'which is to ask questions', 247.260, 249.300, NULL),
  (76213, 201, 'and in particular, to ask
a certain type of question,', 249.340, 251.860, NULL),
  (76214, 201, 'a special question,
what’s known as a “deep question.”', 251.900, 255.020, NULL),
  (76215, 201, 'A deep question is something
that invites us to talk about our values', 255.060, 258.340, NULL),
  (76216, 201, 'or our beliefs or our experiences.', 258.340, 261.180, NULL),
  (76217, 201, 'And that can sound a little intimidating,', 261.220, 263.180, NULL),
  (76218, 201, 'but it''s actually much easier
than it sounds.', 263.220, 265.340, NULL),
  (76219, 201, 'For instance, instead
of asking someone, where do you work?', 265.340, 268.220, NULL),
  (76220, 201, 'You could ask them,
what do you love about your job?', 268.220, 270.700, NULL),
  (76221, 201, 'Instead of asking someone,
where did you go to high school?', 270.740, 273.540, NULL),
  (76222, 201, 'You could say, what was high school like?', 273.580, 276.020, NULL),
  (76223, 201, 'What did you learn there?', 276.060, 277.940, NULL),
  (76224, 201, 'What changed you there?', 278.460, 280.020, NULL),
  (76225, 201, 'Put differently, instead of asking
about the facts of someone''s life,', 281.020, 285.500, NULL),
  (76226, 201, 'we should ask them
how they feel about their life.', 285.500, 288.100, NULL),
  (76227, 201, 'Because when we do,', 288.820, 290.300, NULL),
  (76228, 201, 'they tend to reveal to us
who they really are.', 290.340, 293.660, NULL),
  (76229, 201, 'They tend to tell us what they want,
not only out of this conversation,', 294.220, 297.620, NULL),
  (76230, 201, 'but how they hope that we''ll see them
and how they want to see us,', 297.660, 300.820, NULL),
  (76231, 201, 'what matters to them most.', 300.860, 303.180, NULL),
  (76232, 201, 'In fact, what studies show us
is that this is so powerful', 303.220, 305.980, NULL),
  (76233, 201, 'because these kinds of questions
allow us to be vulnerable.', 306.020, 310.300, NULL),
  (76234, 201, 'And vulnerability
and reciprocal vulnerability --', 310.300, 313.740, NULL),
  (76235, 201, 'when we hear vulnerability
and we become vulnerable in return --', 313.780, 317.460, NULL),
  (76236, 201, 'is the key to allowing us
to connect with other people.', 317.500, 321.100, NULL),
  (76237, 201, 'And to explain how this works,
I want to tell you a story about this guy,', 321.820, 325.260, NULL),
  (76238, 201, 'Dr. Behfar Ehdaie.', 325.260, 326.580, NULL),
  (76239, 201, 'Dr. Ehdaie is a cancer surgeon
in New York City.', 326.580, 329.860, NULL),
  (76240, 201, 'He specializes in prostate cancer,', 329.900, 332.340, NULL),
  (76241, 201, 'removing prostate tumor,', 332.380, 334.460, NULL),
  (76242, 201, 'removing cancer tumors from prostates.', 334.500, 337.220, NULL),
  (76243, 201, 'And he has this kind of interesting job', 337.260, 340.180, NULL),
  (76244, 201, 'because every single day a patient
will come into his office asking,', 340.220, 343.820, NULL),
  (76245, 201, 'he thinks, for medical advice.', 343.860, 345.580, NULL),
  (76246, 201, 'And what he will tell them
is: “You should not get surgery.”', 345.580, 349.700, NULL),
  (76247, 201, 'The prostate is located
so close to the nerves', 350.060, 354.260, NULL),
  (76248, 201, 'that control urination
and sexual function,', 354.260, 356.460, NULL),
  (76249, 201, 'that it''s a relatively risky surgery.', 356.500, 360.540, NULL),
  (76250, 201, 'And what''s more, most prostate tumors,
they grow very, very slowly.', 360.580, 364.500, NULL),
  (76251, 201, 'It''s actually one of the slowest-growing
forms of cancer in existence.', 364.540, 367.980, NULL),
  (76252, 201, 'There''s a saying among doctors
that if you have an old patient', 368.020, 371.100, NULL),
  (76253, 201, 'with prostate cancer,', 371.140, 372.340, NULL),
  (76254, 201, 'he''s going to die of old age
before the cancer kills him.', 372.340, 375.900, NULL),
  (76255, 201, 'And so Dr. Ehdaie would have
these patients come in', 375.940, 378.580, NULL),
  (76256, 201, 'and he would tell them,
"I don''t think you should do anything.', 378.620, 381.540, NULL),
  (76257, 201, 'In fact, I think you should do
active surveillance.', 381.540, 384.020, NULL),
  (76258, 201, 'We''re going to take a blood sample
every six months,', 384.060, 386.500, NULL),
  (76259, 201, 'we''re going to do a biopsy
every two years,', 386.540, 388.540, NULL),
  (76260, 201, 'and if the tumor seems
to change, we''ll do an MRI,', 388.580, 390.940, NULL),
  (76261, 201, 'And if we have to, we can do the surgery.', 390.940, 392.900, NULL),
  (76262, 201, 'But otherwise no radiation, no surgery.', 392.940, 395.020, NULL),
  (76263, 201, 'It''s going to be OK.', 395.060, 396.700, NULL),
  (76264, 201, 'And these patients would listen to him,
and then they would go home,', 397.260, 400.700, NULL),
  (76265, 201, 'and they would discuss it
with their spouse,', 400.700, 402.860, NULL),
  (76266, 201, 'and then they would walk in the next day
and insist on having the surgery.', 402.900, 406.380, NULL),
  (76267, 201, 'They would say, "I absolutely
want you to cut me open,', 406.420, 408.940, NULL),
  (76268, 201, 'take the tumor out as fast as possible."', 408.980, 410.900, NULL),
  (76269, 201, 'And for Dr. Ehdaie,
this was bewildering, right?', 410.940, 413.180, NULL),
  (76270, 201, 'He thought these would be the easiest
conversations of his life.', 413.180, 416.220, NULL),
  (76271, 201, 'He’s telling people that they don’t
have to have surgery, and he’s a surgeon.', 416.260, 419.900, NULL),
  (76272, 201, 'He told me that, “When this happens
again and again and again,', 419.900, 423.380, NULL),
  (76273, 201, 'you start to realize, this isn''t
a problem with my patients.', 423.420, 427.700, NULL),
  (76274, 201, 'This is a problem with me.', 427.740, 429.980, NULL),
  (76275, 201, 'I''m doing something wrong."', 430.300, 432.100, NULL),
  (76276, 201, 'And so he goes to these folks,', 432.460, 434.580, NULL),
  (76277, 201, 'these professors
at the Harvard Business School,', 434.620, 436.900, NULL),
  (76278, 201, 'and he asks them for advice
on what to do differently.', 436.900, 439.460, NULL),
  (76279, 201, 'And they said, "Look, the biggest
mistake that you''re making', 439.460, 442.300, NULL),
  (76280, 201, 'is you are starting
this conversation all wrong.', 442.340, 444.580, NULL),
  (76281, 201, 'You are starting by assuming', 444.620, 445.980, NULL),
  (76282, 201, 'that the patient walks
into your exam room', 446.020, 448.420, NULL),
  (76283, 201, 'looking for advice
and looking for medical solutions.', 448.420, 451.620, NULL),
  (76284, 201, 'But you don''t know if that''s true.', 451.620, 453.260, NULL),
  (76285, 201, 'You''re not asking them any questions.', 453.300, 455.060, NULL),
  (76286, 201, 'What you need to do is you need
to start asking deep questions."', 455.100, 458.700, NULL),
  (76287, 201, 'So two weeks later,
after having this conversation,', 459.020, 461.420, NULL),
  (76288, 201, 'a 62-year-old man comes
into Dr. Ehdaie’s office', 461.460, 464.060, NULL),
  (76289, 201, 'for the first time.', 464.100, 465.340, NULL),
  (76290, 201, 'He had just gotten his diagnosis
of having prostate cancer.', 465.380, 469.380, NULL),
  (76291, 201, 'And Dr. Ehdaie, instead
of giving him advice,', 469.900, 472.020, NULL),
  (76292, 201, 'instead of telling him
what he ought to do,', 472.020, 474.020, NULL),
  (76293, 201, 'he asks the question,', 474.020, 475.860, NULL),
  (76294, 201, '"What does this cancer
diagnosis mean to you?"', 475.900, 479.740, NULL),
  (76295, 201, 'And the man starts talking,', 481.100, 482.740, NULL),
  (76296, 201, 'about how his father had died
when he was 17 years old.', 482.780, 486.660, NULL),
  (76297, 201, 'And this had just been so hard for him,', 487.060, 489.540, NULL),
  (76298, 201, 'and it had been so hard for his mom.', 489.580, 491.380, NULL),
  (76299, 201, 'And then he starts talking
about his grandchildren', 491.420, 493.780, NULL),
  (76300, 201, 'and his fears for the world
they''re inheriting,', 493.780, 496.020, NULL),
  (76301, 201, 'what with climate change
and everything else that''s going on.', 496.060, 498.980, NULL),
  (76302, 201, 'Dr. Ehdaie had expected this guy
to at least bring up cancer,', 499.020, 502.620, NULL),
  (76303, 201, 'to at least mention mortality or pain,', 502.660, 506.020, NULL),
  (76304, 201, 'but it never came up.', 506.060, 507.580, NULL),
  (76305, 201, 'And at that moment, Dr. Ehdaie realized,', 508.460, 510.380, NULL),
  (76306, 201, 'because he had asked this deep question,', 510.380, 512.340, NULL),
  (76307, 201, 'that this man wanted
to have an emotional conversation.', 512.340, 515.500, NULL),
  (76308, 201, 'He wanted to talk about how do we feel?', 515.540, 518.660, NULL),
  (76309, 201, 'He needed to be hugged.', 518.700, 520.340, NULL),
  (76310, 201, 'And so, Dr. Ehdaie didn’t
actually hug him.', 520.860, 523.140, NULL),
  (76311, 201, 'But he did the verbal equivalent.', 523.180, 524.860, NULL),
  (76312, 201, 'And then Dr. Ehdaie says,', 524.860, 526.980, NULL),
  (76313, 201, '"Look, do you mind?', 527.020, 528.740, NULL),
  (76314, 201, 'There''s some medical options
I''d like to talk over with you.', 529.140, 532.180, NULL),
  (76315, 201, 'Is that OK?"', 532.180, 533.380, NULL),
  (76316, 201, 'And they move into a practical
conversation together,', 533.940, 536.500, NULL),
  (76317, 201, 'and within seven more minutes,', 536.500, 538.020, NULL),
  (76318, 201, 'the man decides to do active
surveillance and never looks back.', 538.060, 541.020, NULL),
  (76319, 201, 'Dr. Ehdaie’s patients overwhelmingly
now opt for active surveillance,', 541.060, 545.380, NULL),
  (76320, 201, 'his advice, because of this approach.', 545.380, 547.820, NULL),
  (76321, 201, 'And the thing is, we can do this
in any conversation.', 548.580, 551.580, NULL),
  (76322, 201, 'It doesn''t have to be
an important conversation,', 551.580, 553.820, NULL),
  (76323, 201, 'it doesn''t have to be life or death.', 553.860, 555.620, NULL),
  (76324, 201, 'We can always connect more and better', 555.620, 559.260, NULL),
  (76325, 201, 'and in a really profound way
with the person that we''re speaking to,', 559.300, 562.540, NULL),
  (76326, 201, 'if we want to.', 562.540, 563.820, NULL),
  (76327, 201, 'Which brings me back to that experiment.', 564.860, 567.060, NULL),
  (76328, 201, 'So just to remind you
what you''re supposed to do.', 567.100, 569.980, NULL),
  (76329, 201, 'Walk out of the room,
find a stranger, ask them,', 569.980, 572.540, NULL),
  (76330, 201, '"When was the last time you cried
in front of someone?"', 572.580, 575.300, NULL),
  (76331, 201, 'And then as soon as they answer,
you answer the question yourself,', 575.300, 578.420, NULL),
  (76332, 201, 'and you tell them when you last cried. ', 578.420, 580.300, NULL),
  (76333, 201, 'Let me just say, this experiment
has been done thousands', 580.300, 583.180, NULL),
  (76334, 201, 'and thousands of times,', 583.220, 584.380, NULL),
  (76335, 201, 'most notably by a guy named Nick Epley', 584.380, 586.220, NULL),
  (76336, 201, 'at the University of Chicago.', 586.260, 587.660, NULL),
  (76337, 201, 'People hate this experiment.', 587.700, 590.340, NULL),
  (76338, 201, 'Nobody who participates
comes in and is like,', 590.380, 593.460, NULL),
  (76339, 201, '"This is going to be a great time."', 593.500, 595.180, NULL),
  (76340, 201, 'Instead, what they say is,
"I do not want to do this.', 595.540, 598.580, NULL),
  (76341, 201, 'This sounds terrible."', 598.620, 600.540, NULL),
  (76342, 201, 'But they’re in an experiment,', 600.980, 602.380, NULL),
  (76343, 201, 'and they basically have to do it,
right, they''re in the room.', 602.420, 605.300, NULL),
  (76344, 201, 'They get paired with a partner,
they ask the question, ask and answer.', 605.340, 608.620, NULL),
  (76345, 201, 'And then Nick Epley afterwards
asks them, what was that like?', 608.660, 613.020, NULL),
  (76346, 201, 'And what people say are things like,', 613.620, 615.340, NULL),
  (76347, 201, '"Oh my gosh, I felt
so connected to that person,', 615.380, 617.620, NULL),
  (76348, 201, 'more connected than to people
in other conversations in a while.', 617.660, 621.780, NULL),
  (76349, 201, 'I felt more caring towards them', 621.820, 623.820, NULL),
  (76350, 201, 'and I felt like they were
really caring about me."', 623.860, 626.300, NULL),
  (76351, 201, 'So why is this so powerful?', 626.300, 628.380, NULL),
  (76352, 201, 'Why is asking this question,', 628.420, 630.380, NULL),
  (76353, 201, 'why does it help us connect so well?', 630.420, 632.620, NULL),
  (76354, 201, 'Because it''s a deep question.', 632.620, 634.660, NULL),
  (76355, 201, 'It allows us to say something real.', 634.700, 637.060, NULL),
  (76356, 201, 'And when we ask deep questions,', 638.220, 639.780, NULL),
  (76357, 201, 'we figure out which of the three
conversations we''re in,', 639.820, 643.140, NULL),
  (76358, 201, 'what we''re talking about,', 643.140, 644.340, NULL),
  (76359, 201, 'what everyone really wants
out of this dialogue.', 644.380, 647.940, NULL),
  (76360, 201, 'And that is how we connect
with each other.', 647.940, 650.100, NULL),
  (76361, 201, 'We are living through a time
of polarization and division.', 651.380, 656.740, NULL),
  (76362, 201, 'We have forgotten how
to have conversations.', 657.460, 660.820, NULL),
  (76363, 201, 'But there''s a science to it.', 662.700, 664.660, NULL),
  (76364, 201, 'There are these folks who are known
as supercommunicators.', 665.460, 668.460, NULL),
  (76365, 201, 'They are not special,', 668.460, 670.460, NULL),
  (76366, 201, 'they’re not more charismatic,', 670.500, 671.900, NULL),
  (76367, 201, 'or they''re not more outgoing
than anyone else.', 671.900, 675.660, NULL),
  (76368, 201, 'They''ve just learned skills
that allow us to connect with others.', 675.660, 679.660, NULL),
  (76369, 201, 'And they''re skills
that all of us can learn.', 680.060, 682.700, NULL),
  (76370, 201, 'And that feeling you get
after a wonderful conversation,', 683.300, 686.620, NULL),
  (76371, 201, 'that glow that you experience,', 686.660, 689.580, NULL),
  (76372, 201, 'our brains have evolved to give us that,', 689.620, 693.900, NULL),
  (76373, 201, 'to crave connection.', 693.940, 695.700, NULL),
  (76374, 201, 'So I hope you go out,', 696.620, 698.340, NULL),
  (76375, 201, 'I hope you find a stranger,', 698.380, 700.020, NULL),
  (76376, 201, 'and I hope you tell them all
about the last time you cried', 700.020, 702.860, NULL),
  (76377, 201, 'in front of another person.', 702.860, 704.220, NULL),
  (76378, 201, 'And then tell me how it went.', 704.220, 705.660, NULL),
  (76379, 201, 'Thanks.', 705.980, 707.140, NULL),
  (76380, 202, 'So a couple of years ago,', 4.292, 6.002, NULL),
  (76381, 202, 'a woman I know who I''ll call Maya,', 6.044, 9.005, NULL),
  (76382, 202, 'went through a lot of big changes
in a short amount of time.', 9.047, 12.175, NULL),
  (76383, 202, 'She got married.', 12.217, 13.385, NULL),
  (76384, 202, 'She and her husband moved
for his job to a new city', 13.426, 16.096, NULL),
  (76385, 202, 'where she didn’t know anyone.', 16.096, 17.764, NULL),
  (76386, 202, 'She started a new role working from home,', 17.806, 20.392, NULL),
  (76387, 202, 'all while managing her dad''s
new diagnosis of dementia.', 20.433, 23.979, NULL),
  (76388, 202, 'And to manage the stress
of all this change,', 24.688, 27.566, NULL),
  (76389, 202, 'Maya doubled down on her physical
and mental health.', 27.607, 30.944, NULL),
  (76390, 202, 'She exercised almost every day,', 30.986, 32.779, NULL),
  (76391, 202, 'she ate healthy foods,', 32.821, 34.197, NULL),
  (76392, 202, 'she went to therapy once a week.', 34.239, 35.991, NULL),
  (76393, 202, 'And these actions really helped.', 36.324, 37.951, NULL),
  (76394, 202, 'Her body got stronger.', 37.993, 39.661, NULL),
  (76395, 202, 'Her mind got more resilient,
but only up to a point.', 39.703, 43.540, NULL),
  (76396, 202, 'She was still struggling,', 44.332, 45.875, NULL),
  (76397, 202, 'often losing sleep
in the middle of the night,', 45.875, 48.795, NULL),
  (76398, 202, 'feeling unfocused,
unmotivated during the day.', 48.795, 51.256, NULL),
  (76399, 202, 'Maya was doing everything
that doctors typically tell us to do', 51.298, 55.051, NULL),
  (76400, 202, 'to be physically and mentally healthy,', 55.051, 57.721, NULL),
  (76401, 202, 'and yet something was missing.', 57.762, 59.681, NULL),
  (76402, 202, 'What if I told you
that what was missing for Maya', 61.057, 63.935, NULL),
  (76403, 202, 'is also missing for billions
of people around the world,', 63.977, 67.939, NULL),
  (76404, 202, 'and that it might be missing for you?', 67.939, 69.858, NULL),
  (76405, 202, 'What if I told you that not having it
undermines our other efforts', 70.859, 74.863, NULL),
  (76406, 202, 'to be healthy and can even
shorten your lifespan?', 74.904, 78.283, NULL),
  (76407, 202, 'I’ve been studying this for over a decade,', 79.951, 82.162, NULL),
  (76408, 202, 'and I''ve discovered
that the traditional way we think', 82.203, 85.206, NULL),
  (76409, 202, 'about health is incomplete.', 85.248, 87.959, NULL),
  (76410, 202, 'By thinking of our health
as primarily physical and mental,', 88.460, 92.213, NULL),
  (76411, 202, 'we overlook what I believe
is the greatest challenge', 92.255, 95.800, NULL),
  (76412, 202, 'and the greatest opportunity of our time.', 95.842, 98.637, NULL),
  (76413, 202, 'Social health.', 99.304, 100.972, NULL),
  (76414, 202, 'While physical health is about our bodies', 101.681, 104.267, NULL),
  (76415, 202, 'and mental health is about our minds,', 104.309, 106.436, NULL),
  (76416, 202, 'social health is about our relationships.', 106.478, 110.190, NULL),
  (76417, 202, 'And if you haven''t heard this term before,', 110.649, 112.692, NULL),
  (76418, 202, 'that''s because it hasn''t yet
made its way into mainstream vocabulary,', 112.692, 116.696, NULL),
  (76419, 202, 'yet it is equally important.', 116.738, 119.324, NULL),
  (76420, 202, 'Maya didn''t yet have a sense
of community in her new home.', 120.158, 123.453, NULL),
  (76421, 202, 'She wasn''t seeing her family,', 123.453, 125.205, NULL),
  (76422, 202, 'or her friends or her coworkers
in person anymore,', 125.246, 128.291, NULL),
  (76423, 202, 'and she often went weeks only spending
quality time with her husband.', 128.333, 132.045, NULL),
  (76424, 202, 'Her story shows us
that we can''t be fully healthy,', 132.045, 136.424, NULL),
  (76425, 202, 'we can’t thrive, if we take
care of our bodies and our minds,', 136.466, 140.470, NULL),
  (76426, 202, 'but not our relationships.', 140.470, 142.305, NULL),
  (76427, 202, 'Similar to Maya,', 143.723, 145.308, NULL),
  (76428, 202, 'hundreds of millions of people
around the world go weeks at a time', 145.308, 149.396, NULL),
  (76429, 202, 'without talking to a single friend
or family member.', 149.396, 153.149, NULL),
  (76430, 202, 'Globally, one in four people feel lonely.', 154.275, 157.737, NULL),
  (76431, 202, 'And 20 percent of adults worldwide
don''t feel like they have anyone', 158.488, 162.534, NULL),
  (76432, 202, 'they can reach out to for support.', 162.575, 164.619, NULL),
  (76433, 202, 'Think about that.', 165.245, 166.413, NULL),
  (76434, 202, 'One in five people you encounter
may feel like they have no one.', 166.746, 170.917, NULL),
  (76435, 202, 'This is more than heartbreaking.', 172.377, 174.921, NULL),
  (76436, 202, 'It''s also a public health crisis.', 174.963, 177.215, NULL),
  (76437, 202, 'Disconnection triggers stress in the body.', 177.549, 180.176, NULL),
  (76438, 202, 'It weakens people''s immune systems.', 180.218, 182.887, NULL),
  (76439, 202, 'It puts them at a risk, greater risk,
of stroke, heart disease, diabetes,', 182.929, 188.059, NULL),
  (76440, 202, 'dementia, depression and early death.', 188.059, 191.146, NULL),
  (76441, 202, 'Social health is essential for longevity.', 191.646, 194.607, NULL),
  (76442, 202, 'So you might be wondering,', 196.025, 197.402, NULL),
  (76443, 202, 'what does it look like
to be socially healthy?', 197.444, 199.863, NULL),
  (76444, 202, 'What does that even mean?', 199.863, 201.239, NULL),
  (76445, 202, 'Well it’s about developing
close relationships', 201.531, 204.451, NULL),
  (76446, 202, 'with your family, your friends,
your partner, yourself.', 204.451, 208.079, NULL),
  (76447, 202, 'It''s about having regular interaction
with your coworkers, your neighbors.', 208.580, 212.917, NULL),
  (76448, 202, 'It''s about feeling like
you belong to a community.', 212.917, 215.503, NULL),
  (76449, 202, 'Being socially healthy is about
having the right quantity', 216.254, 220.091, NULL),
  (76450, 202, 'and quality of connection for you.', 220.133, 222.594, NULL),
  (76451, 202, 'And Maya''s story is one example', 223.845, 225.638, NULL),
  (76452, 202, 'of how social health challenges come up.', 225.680, 228.183, NULL),
  (76453, 202, 'In my work, I hear many others.', 228.224, 230.560, NULL),
  (76454, 202, 'Stories like Jay,', 230.602, 232.020, NULL),
  (76455, 202, 'a freshman in college', 232.061, 233.438, NULL),
  (76456, 202, 'who’s eager to get involved in campus', 233.480, 236.149, NULL),
  (76457, 202, 'yet is having a hard time
fitting in with people in his dorm', 236.149, 238.985, NULL),
  (76458, 202, 'and often feels homesick.', 239.027, 240.612, NULL),
  (76459, 202, 'Or Serena and Ali, a couple
juggling the chaos of young kids', 241.154, 244.991, NULL),
  (76460, 202, 'with demanding jobs,', 245.033, 246.576, NULL),
  (76461, 202, 'they rarely have time to see friends', 246.618, 248.411, NULL),
  (76462, 202, 'or spend time one-on-one.', 248.411, 250.205, NULL),
  (76463, 202, 'Or Henry, recently retired,', 250.997, 253.124, NULL),
  (76464, 202, 'who cherishes time with his spouse', 253.166, 255.460, NULL),
  (76465, 202, 'and yet feels untethered
without his team anymore', 255.460, 258.963, NULL),
  (76466, 202, 'and wishes he could see his kids
and grandkids more often.', 258.963, 262.008, NULL),
  (76467, 202, 'These stories show that social health
is relevant to each of us', 262.467, 267.180, NULL),
  (76468, 202, 'at every life stage.', 267.222, 269.140, NULL),
  (76469, 202, 'So if you''re not sure where to start,', 269.766, 271.726, NULL),
  (76470, 202, 'try the 5-3-1 guideline from my book.', 271.726, 274.521, NULL),
  (76471, 202, 'It goes like this.', 274.562, 275.855, NULL),
  (76472, 202, 'Aim to interact with five
different people each week,', 275.897, 279.442, NULL),
  (76473, 202, 'to strengthen at least three
close relationships overall,', 279.484, 283.488, NULL),
  (76474, 202, 'and to spend one hour a day connecting.', 283.530, 286.449, NULL),
  (76475, 202, 'Let''s dig into these.', 286.449, 287.826, NULL),
  (76476, 202, 'So first, interact with five
different people each week.', 288.201, 292.038, NULL),
  (76477, 202, 'Just like eating a variety of vegetables', 292.080, 294.457, NULL),
  (76478, 202, 'and other food groups is more nutritious,', 294.499, 297.085, NULL),
  (76479, 202, 'research has shown that interacting
with a variety of people', 297.085, 300.547, NULL),
  (76480, 202, 'is more rewarding.', 300.547, 301.840, NULL),
  (76481, 202, 'So your five could include close
loved ones, casual acquaintances,', 301.881, 305.844, NULL),
  (76482, 202, 'even complete strangers.', 305.844, 307.095, NULL),
  (76483, 202, 'In fact, in one study that I love,', 307.136, 309.347, NULL),
  (76484, 202, 'people who just smiled, made eye contact', 309.347, 311.766, NULL),
  (76485, 202, 'and chit-chatted with a barista,', 311.766, 313.726, NULL),
  (76486, 202, 'felt happier and a greater
sense of belonging', 313.726, 316.396, NULL),
  (76487, 202, 'than people who just rushed
to get their coffee and go.', 316.437, 319.315, NULL),
  (76488, 202, 'Next, strengthen at least
three close relationships.', 320.066, 323.778, NULL),
  (76489, 202, 'OK, we''ve all heard of a to-do list,', 324.237, 326.614, NULL),
  (76490, 202, 'but I would like to invite you
to write a to-love list.', 326.656, 330.410, NULL),
  (76491, 202, 'Who matters most to you?', 331.327, 333.162, NULL),
  (76492, 202, 'Who can you be yourself with?', 333.204, 335.290, NULL),
  (76493, 202, 'Make sure that you invest', 335.331, 337.041, NULL),
  (76494, 202, 'in the names of at least three
of the people that you write down.', 337.041, 340.628, NULL),
  (76495, 202, 'By scheduling regular time together,', 340.670, 342.797, NULL),
  (76496, 202, 'by showing a genuine interest
in their lives,', 342.797, 345.508, NULL),
  (76497, 202, 'and also by opening up', 345.550, 346.885, NULL),
  (76498, 202, 'about the experiences
that you''re going through.', 346.926, 349.262, NULL),
  (76499, 202, 'And I''m often asked,
does it have to be in person?', 349.637, 352.181, NULL),
  (76500, 202, 'Does texting count?', 352.223, 353.725, NULL),
  (76501, 202, 'Studies have shown
that face-to-face is ideal,', 354.309, 356.728, NULL),
  (76502, 202, 'so do that whenever possible.', 356.769, 358.313, NULL),
  (76503, 202, 'But there are absolutely still benefits
to staying connected virtually.', 358.313, 362.108, NULL),
  (76504, 202, 'And last, spend one hour a day
on meaningful connection.', 362.901, 367.488, NULL),
  (76505, 202, 'OK, if you''re an introvert,
right now you''re probably thinking', 367.488, 370.742, NULL),
  (76506, 202, 'one hour sounds like a lot.', 370.742, 373.036, NULL),
  (76507, 202, 'I get it.', 373.494, 374.704, NULL),
  (76508, 202, 'It might be surprising,
but I''m actually also an introvert.', 374.704, 377.999, NULL),
  (76509, 202, 'However, keep in mind that just like
getting eight hours of sleep at night,', 378.791, 382.837, NULL),
  (76510, 202, 'the exact amount
that''s right for you personally', 382.879, 385.590, NULL),
  (76511, 202, 'might be higher or lower.', 385.590, 387.300, NULL),
  (76512, 202, 'But if you are thinking that one hour
