  (123360, 217, '- So I don''t want to give the impression though
that the surreal numbers are not widely studied,', 10596.184, 10600.083, NULL),
  (123361, 217, 'because there are thousands
of people who are...', 10600.124, 10601.794, NULL),
  (123362, 217, '- Sure', 10601.844, 10601.984, NULL),
  (123363, 217, '- ...studying it. In fact, Philip Ehrlich,
who is one of the world experts on', 10602.003, 10605.964, NULL),
  (123364, 217, 'the surreal numbers, mentioned to
me once that Conway was his own', 10605.984, 10609.964, NULL),
  (123365, 217, 'worst enemy with regard to
that very issue, because', 10610.003, 10613.083, NULL),
  (123366, 217, 'in the Conway style,
everything is a game. And he', 10614.024, 10617.644, NULL),
  (123367, 217, 'treated the surreal numbers
as a kind of plaything, a', 10617.724, 10620.503, NULL),
  (123368, 217, 'toy, and maybe that makes people', 10620.624, 10624.364, NULL),
  (123369, 217, 'not take it seriously.
Although my view is that it is', 10624.384, 10627.744, NULL),
  (123370, 217, 'extremely serious, useful, and profound,', 10627.804, 10631.604, NULL),
  (123371, 217, 'and I''ve been writing a whole series
of essays on the surreal numbers for', 10631.684, 10635.624, NULL),
  (123372, 217, 'my Substack at Infinitely More. And
I just find the whole subject so', 10635.664, 10640.824, NULL),
  (123373, 217, 'fascinating and beautiful.
I mean, it''s true. I''m not', 10640.904, 10644.508, NULL),
  (123374, 217, 'applying it in engineering, which maybe
was part of this Conway ambition.', 10644.548, 10650.568, NULL),
  (123375, 217, '- And I just wanted to,
before I forget, mention', 10650.648, 10652.728, NULL),
  (123376, 217, 'the Conway, turning everything
into a game. It is a fascinating', 10654.248, 10658.168, NULL),
  (123377, 217, 'point that I didn''t quite think about,
which I think the Game of Life is just an', 10658.208, 10661.888, NULL),
  (123378, 217, 'example of exploration of cellular automata.
I think cellular automata is one of the', 10661.928, 10665.788, NULL),
  (123379, 217, 'most incredible, complicated,
fascinating... It feels like an', 10665.828, 10669.788, NULL),
  (123380, 217, 'open door into a world we have not
quite yet explored. And it''s such', 10669.888, 10673.788, NULL),
  (123381, 217, 'a beautiful illustration of that world,
the Game of Life, but calling it a', 10673.908, 10677.108, NULL),
  (123382, 217, 'game, maybe life balances it,
''cause that''s your powerful word,', 10677.148, 10681.088, NULL),
  (123383, 217, 'but it''s not quite a
game. It''s a fascinating', 10681.128, 10684.328, NULL),
  (123384, 217, 'invitation to an incredibly complicated
and fascinating mathematical', 10684.408, 10688.218, NULL),
  (123385, 217, 'world. I think every time I see
cellular automata and the fact that', 10688.268, 10692.248, NULL),
  (123386, 217, 'we don''t quite have mathematical
tools to make sense', 10692.268, 10696.188, NULL),
  (123387, 217, 'of that world, it fills me with awe.
Speaking of a thousand years from', 10696.208, 10700.168, NULL),
  (123388, 217, 'now, it feels like that is a world
we might make some progress on.', 10700.228, 10703.988, NULL),
  (123389, 217, '- The Game of Life is a sort of
playground for computably undecidable', 10703.998, 10707.567, NULL),
  (123390, 217, 'questions because in fact,
you can prove that the', 10707.628, 10711.528, NULL),
  (123391, 217, 'question of whether a given
cell will ever become alive', 10711.608, 10715.568, NULL),
  (123392, 217, 'is computably undecidable. In other words,', 10716.548, 10719.288, NULL),
  (123393, 217, 'given a configuration and you ask,
"Will this particular cell ever', 10719.348, 10723.328, NULL),
  (123394, 217, 'you know, be alive?" "...in the
evolution?" And you can prove that that', 10723.348, 10729.618, NULL),
  (123395, 217, 'question is equivalent to the
Halting Problem. It''s computably', 10729.708, 10733.168, NULL),
  (123396, 217, 'undecidable. It''s semi-decidable
in the sense that if it will', 10733.208, 10737.198, NULL),
  (123397, 217, 'become alive, then you will know it at a
finite stage because you could just run the', 10737.248, 10741.008, NULL),
  (123398, 217, 'Game of Life algorithm and let
it run. And if it ever did come', 10741.688, 10745.468, NULL),
  (123399, 217, 'alive, you could say, "Yeah, it was alive."
But if you''ve run it for a thousand', 10745.508, 10749.388, NULL),
  (123400, 217, 'years and it hasn''t come alive yet,
then you don''t necessarily seem to', 10749.468, 10753.388, NULL),
  (123401, 217, 'have any basis for saying, "No, it won''t
ever come alive" if the behavior was very', 10753.428, 10756.988, NULL),
  (123402, 217, 'complicated.', 10757.008, 10758.048, NULL),
  (123403, 217, 'Maybe if you have a complete understanding of
the evolution of the behavior, then you can say', 10758.088, 10761.968, NULL),
  (123404, 217, 'no, but you can prove you won''t
always have that understanding-', 10762.008, 10765.228, NULL),
  (123405, 217, '... precisely because the problem is
equivalent to the halting problem.', 10765.238, 10768.088, NULL),
  (123406, 217, '- And nevertheless, when you sit back
and look and visualize the thing, some', 10768.148, 10771.808, NULL),
  (123407, 217, 'little mini cellular automata
civilizations are born and die quickly,', 10771.848, 10775.797, NULL),
  (123408, 217, 'and some are very predictable
and boring, but some have this', 10775.848, 10779.758, NULL),
  (123409, 217, 'rich incredible complexity. And maybe that', 10779.808, 10783.428, NULL),
  (123410, 217, 'speaks to a thing I wanted to ask on
the halting problem and decidability.', 10783.528, 10788.858, NULL),
  (123411, 217, 'You''ve mentioned this thing where if
you understand the program deeply, you', 10790.028, 10793.948, NULL),
  (123412, 217, 'might be able to say something. So
can we say something interesting', 10794.008, 10797.788, NULL),
  (123413, 217, 'about maybe once statistically
how many programs', 10797.888, 10801.648, NULL),
  (123414, 217, 'we know something about in terms of whether
they halt or not? Or what does it mean to', 10802.648, 10806.268, NULL),
  (123415, 217, 'understand a program deeply enough
to be able to make a prediction?', 10806.288, 10811.248, NULL),
  (123416, 217, '- The main lesson of
computability theory in my view', 10811.268, 10815.308, NULL),
  (123417, 217, 'is that it''s never the case
that you can have a thorough', 10816.708, 10822.188, NULL),
  (123418, 217, 'understanding of the behavior
of a program by looking at the', 10822.268, 10825.808, NULL),
  (123419, 217, 'program, and that the content
of what you learn from', 10825.828, 10829.778, NULL),
  (123420, 217, 'a program, I mean, in the most general
case, is always obtained just by', 10829.788, 10833.648, NULL),
  (123421, 217, 'running it and looking
at the behavior. And the', 10833.728, 10837.368, NULL),
  (123422, 217, 'proof of that is there''s a theorem
called Rice''s Theorem, which', 10837.508, 10841.408, NULL),
  (123423, 217, 'makes that idea completely robust. But I
want to just take a little detour towards', 10841.468, 10848.408, NULL),
  (123424, 217, 'another question riffing
on something that you just', 10848.468, 10851.768, NULL),
  (123425, 217, 'said. Namely, one can ask the question,', 10851.808, 10855.748, NULL),
  (123426, 217, 'What is the behavior of
a random program? So you', 10855.868, 10859.858, NULL),
  (123427, 217, 'have some formal computing language, you
know, and you want to, you know, look', 10859.908, 10863.898, NULL),
  (123428, 217, 'at the collection of all programs of a
certain size. Maybe there''s only finitely', 10863.928, 10867.628, NULL),
  (123429, 217, 'many. And', 10867.688, 10869.188, NULL),
  (123430, 217, 'can you say something about the behavior
of a randomly chosen one, like with a', 10870.108, 10873.988, NULL),
  (123431, 217, 'certain likelihood it will have a certain
behavior? And the answer turns out', 10874.008, 10877.978, NULL),
  (123432, 217, 'to be extremely interesting.
Once years ago,', 10878.008, 10881.628, NULL),
  (123433, 217, 'Alexey Myasnikov asked me a
question. He had this concept of a', 10881.668, 10885.308, NULL),
  (123434, 217, 'decision problem with a black
hole, and what that means is', 10885.408, 10888.388, NULL),
  (123435, 217, 'it''s a decision problem which is
possibly difficult in the worst', 10889.408, 10892.968, NULL),
  (123436, 217, 'case, but the difficulty
was concentrated in a very', 10893.008, 10896.788, NULL),
  (123437, 217, 'tiny region called the black hole. And
outside of that black hole, it was very easy.', 10896.848, 10902.668, NULL),
  (123438, 217, 'And so, for example, this kind of
problem is a terrible problem to use if', 10902.677, 10906.128, NULL),
  (123439, 217, 'you''re basing your encryption
scheme, you know, you don''t', 10906.168, 10910.118, NULL),
  (123440, 217, 'want to use a black hole problem
because if someone can rob the bank', 10910.128, 10913.908, NULL),
  (123441, 217, '95% of the time, you know,
then that''s not what you want,', 10914.128, 10917.988, NULL),
  (123442, 217, 'or even any non-trivial percent
of the time is too dangerous.', 10918.008, 10921.508, NULL),
  (123443, 217, 'So you don''t want to use problems that
are, you know, almost every case is easily', 10921.528, 10928.088, NULL),
  (123444, 217, 'solved as the basis of your
encryption. And the question Alexey', 10928.388, 10932.188, NULL),
  (123445, 217, 'asked me was, "Does the
halting problem have a black', 10932.228, 10935.768, NULL),
  (123446, 217, 'hole?" You know? And so if we
take, say, the standard model of', 10935.828, 10939.668, NULL),
  (123447, 217, 'Turing machines, it''s one-way
infinite tape with zeros', 10939.688, 10943.618, NULL),
  (123448, 217, 'and ones on the tape and so on, the head
moving back and forth, and you know, it', 10943.628, 10947.468, NULL),
  (123449, 217, 'stops when it gets into the
halt state, then it turns', 10947.548, 10951.448, NULL),
  (123450, 217, 'out we proved that there is
a black hole. And what that', 10951.548, 10955.328, NULL),
  (123451, 217, 'means is there''s a computer
procedure that decides correctly', 10955.368, 10958.968, NULL),
  (123452, 217, 'almost every instance of the halting problem.
Even though the halting problem is not', 10959.288, 10963.008, NULL),
  (123453, 217, 'decidable, we can decide almost
every instance. So, more', 10963.068, 10967.008, NULL),
  (123454, 217, 'precisely, there''s a collection
of Turing machine programs', 10967.028, 10970.608, NULL),
  (123455, 217, 'such that we can easily decide
whether a program''s in that', 10971.608, 10975.418, NULL),
  (123456, 217, 'collection or not. And for the
programs in the collection,', 10975.488, 10979.228, NULL),
  (123457, 217, 'we can decide the halting
problem for those programs', 10979.328, 10982.368, NULL),
  (123458, 217, 'easily. And furthermore,
almost every program is', 10982.568, 10986.428, NULL),
  (123459, 217, 'in the collection in the sense that as
the number of states goes to, you know,', 10986.528, 10990.458, NULL),
  (123460, 217, 'becomes large, the proportion of
programs in the collection goes to 100%.', 10990.488, 10997.988, NULL),
  (123461, 217, 'So the asymptotic density
of the programs is one.', 10998.428, 11003.028, NULL),
  (123462, 217, 'And the proof was quite fascinating
because it''s one of these situations where', 11003.048, 11006.908, NULL),
  (123463, 217, 'the theorem sounds really surprising, I
think, to many people when I first tell it,', 11006.948, 11010.908, NULL),
  (123464, 217, 'I mean, to computability
experts. Then it''s sort of', 11010.928, 11014.588, NULL),
  (123465, 217, 'intriguing to think that you can solve
almost every instance of a halting', 11014.648, 11018.368, NULL),
  (123466, 217, 'problem. But then when
they hear the proof, it''s', 11018.408, 11022.048, NULL),
  (123467, 217, 'completely a letdown. Unfortunately,
nobody likes the theorem after the proof.', 11022.088, 11027.908, NULL),
  (123468, 217, 'And so the proof is so simple,', 11027.948, 11030.978, NULL),
  (123469, 217, 'though. If you know how a Turing
machine operates, there''s this infinite', 11030.978, 11034.208, NULL),
  (123470, 217, 'paper tape on which the machine writes
zeros and ones, and the head moves', 11034.288, 11037.988, NULL),
  (123471, 217, 'back and forth according to rigid
instructions. And the instructions are', 11038.028, 11041.308, NULL),
  (123472, 217, 'all of the form,', 11041.368, 11045.228, NULL),
  (123473, 217, 'if the machine is in such and such a state
and it''s reading such and such symbol', 11045.967, 11049.908, NULL),
  (123474, 217, 'on the tape, then it should write
this symbol on the tape and it should', 11049.967, 11053.887, NULL),
  (123475, 217, 'change to this new state specified, and
it should either move left or right', 11053.948, 11057.908, NULL),
  (123476, 217, 'as specified. So a program consists of
instructions like that. If you look at,', 11057.967, 11064.608, NULL),
  (123477, 217, 'If you look at a program, you know, one of
the states is the halt state and that''s', 11065.448, 11069.268, NULL),
  (123478, 217, 'when the program halts. But you
can calculate how many programs', 11069.308, 11074.428, NULL),
  (123479, 217, 'don''t have any instruction
that transitions to the halt', 11075.808, 11078.848, NULL),
  (123480, 217, 'state. You can easily
calculate the proportion. And', 11078.967, 11082.848, NULL),
  (123481, 217, 'in the limit, it goes to 1
over E squared, 13 and a half', 11082.887, 11086.848, NULL),
  (123482, 217, 'percent. If you calculate the limit, the', 11086.868, 11090.387, NULL),
  (123483, 217, 'proportion of programs
with end states that don''t', 11090.428, 11094.228, NULL),
  (123484, 217, 'ever halt because they don''t have
any instruction saying halt.', 11094.308, 11098.368, NULL),
  (123485, 217, 'Those programs obviously never halt because
they can''t halt. They don''t have any', 11098.788, 11102.467, NULL),
  (123486, 217, 'instruction that says halt.', 11102.478, 11103.948, NULL),
  (123487, 217, '- So 13% of programs, you could say,', 11104.008, 11106.648, NULL),
  (123488, 217, '- 13%, you can say they don''t halt, because you
just look at them and you can understand them.', 11106.668, 11110.048, NULL),
  (123489, 217, '- There''s no halt state.', 11110.348, 11111.258, NULL),
  (123490, 217, '- There''s no... They never change to
the halt state, so they can''t halt.', 11111.258, 11113.887, NULL),
  (123491, 217, '- I mean, that nevertheless
is beautiful to know.', 11113.928, 11117.188, NULL),
  (123492, 217, '- So that''s a kind of trivial
reason for non-halting, you know.', 11117.228, 11121.028, NULL),
  (123493, 217, 'And when I first made that observation,
I thought, "Okay, this is the proof', 11121.048, 11124.328, NULL),
  (123494, 217, 'strategy." Because we wanted...
I wanted to say at first the', 11124.408, 11128.338, NULL),
  (123495, 217, 'goal was, look, that''s a stupid
reason for a program not to', 11128.408, 11132.248, NULL),
  (123496, 217, 'halt. And I just want to pile up as many
stupid reasons as I can think of...', 11132.288, 11137.198, NULL),
  (123497, 217, '...until it gets more than
50%, and then I can say most.', 11137.198, 11143.458, NULL),
  (123498, 217, '- That was brilliant.', 11143.608, 11143.798, NULL),
  (123499, 217, '- Yeah. That was my goal.', 11143.798, 11144.368, NULL),
  (123500, 217, '- I love this.', 11144.408, 11145.048, NULL),
  (123501, 217, '- Yeah. So we thought more about
it, though, and we hit the', 11145.108, 11148.517, NULL),
  (123502, 217, 'jackpot because we found one
gigantic stupid reason that', 11148.608, 11152.508, NULL),
  (123503, 217, 'converged to 100%. I mean, in the
limit. And so, the stupid reason for a', 11152.568, 11160.288, NULL),
  (123504, 217, 'program not to halt is that,
well, if you think about', 11160.308, 11164.048, NULL),
  (123505, 217, 'the behavior, see, the head is sitting
there. It''s on the leftmost cell of the', 11164.387, 11167.808, NULL),
  (123506, 217, 'tape at the very beginning.
It''s in the start', 11167.848, 11170.648, NULL),
  (123507, 217, 'state, and the head is following an
instruction. And the instruction says,', 11170.748, 11174.528, NULL),
  (123508, 217, '"When you''re in the start state," which it is,
"and you''re reading something on the tape,', 11174.988, 11178.788, NULL),
  (123509, 217, 'then you should write something and you should
change to a new state, and you should either move', 11178.868, 11182.768, NULL),
  (123510, 217, 'left or right." But
half of them move left.', 11182.808, 11186.748, NULL),
  (123511, 217, 'But if you move left and you are
already at the end, then the head falls', 11187.988, 11191.088, NULL),
  (123512, 217, 'off. And so the computation stops
because the head fell off the tape.', 11191.208, 11195.748, NULL),
  (123513, 217, 'That''s a pretty stupid reason. Okay, but
that''s half of them already just like', 11197.168, 11200.628, NULL),
  (123514, 217, 'that. Okay, and then some of them
went right, and they changed to a new', 11200.688, 11204.148, NULL),
  (123515, 217, 'state. And amongst those, you know, the new
state, half of those ones are going left and', 11204.308, 11208.238, NULL),
  (123516, 217, 'half are going right from that
place, and then most of those are', 11208.288, 11212.268, NULL),
  (123517, 217, 'changing to a new state. When there''s a lot of
states, it''s very likely that the next state that you', 11212.308, 11216.028, NULL),
  (123518, 217, 'transition to is new. And so you get this', 11216.048, 11219.837, NULL),
  (123519, 217, 'random walk behavior, if you know what that
means, where half go left and half go right at', 11219.887, 11223.668, NULL),
  (123520, 217, 'each step. And there''s a
theorem due to Pólya which is', 11223.728, 11227.508, NULL),
  (123521, 217, 'called the Pólya recurrence
theorem, which says when you have', 11227.548, 11231.508, NULL),
  (123522, 217, 'a random walk, a one-dimensional
random walk, then it''s very', 11231.548, 11235.028, NULL),
  (123523, 217, 'likely to come back to where you
started. And when that happens for', 11235.108, 11238.848, NULL),
  (123524, 217, 'us, then half of them from that
place fall off on the next step.', 11238.908, 11242.318, NULL),
  (123525, 217, 'And so you can show using
this kind of analysis that', 11243.728, 11247.628, NULL),
  (123526, 217, 'the probability one behavior
of a random Turing machine is', 11248.248, 11252.088, NULL),
  (123527, 217, 'that the head falls off the
tape before it repeats a', 11252.148, 11255.488, NULL),
  (123528, 217, 'state. And that is the stupid proof that', 11255.568, 11259.518, NULL),
  (123529, 217, 'shows how to solve the halting problem. Because when
that happens, we can answer the halting problem', 11259.548, 11262.688, NULL),
  (123530, 217, 'saying, "No, the computation stopped
because the machine crashed, not because', 11262.768, 11266.688, NULL),
  (123531, 217, 'it halted, so therefore it
doesn''t count as halting on some', 11266.728, 11270.458, NULL),
  (123532, 217, 'accounts." Or, you know, if you want to
define that as halting, crashing as halting,', 11270.528, 11274.288, NULL),
  (123533, 217, 'then... But in any case, however it is
that you set up your formalism, you''re', 11274.308, 11278.288, NULL),
  (123534, 217, 'going to be able to answer the
question for the behavior of the', 11278.308, 11281.217, NULL),
  (123535, 217, 'machine when the head falls off.', 11281.217, 11283.887, NULL),
  (123536, 217, '- So statistically, in the limit,
you solve the halting problem.', 11283.967, 11288.208, NULL),
  (123537, 217, '- Yes, exactly. Computably solve it. Yeah.', 11288.268, 11291.578, NULL),
  (123538, 217, '- What do we take from that? Because
you didn''t solve the halting problem.', 11291.928, 11295.308, NULL),
  (123539, 217, '- No, it''s impossible to fully solve...', 11295.408, 11297.688, NULL),
  (123540, 217, '...the halting problem
correctly in all cases.', 11297.698, 11299.818, NULL),
  (123541, 217, '- That''s pretty cool. That''s kind of...
I mean, I don''t know. This is...', 11300.088, 11302.217, NULL),
  (123542, 217, '- It''s a probabilistic way... I mean,
it''s probabilistic in the sense that', 11302.217, 11306.188, NULL),
  (123543, 217, 'we''re solving almost all instances...', 11306.228, 11308.868, NULL),
  (123544, 217, '...computably. There are versions of
this that are maybe more interesting', 11308.878, 11312.788, NULL),
  (123545, 217, 'from the point of view of complexity theory
and actually useful. I mean, there''s the whole', 11312.828, 11316.128, NULL),
  (123546, 217, 'P-NP problem and so on. And
there''s this genre of NP-complete', 11316.268, 11319.868, NULL),
  (123547, 217, 'problems, which are problems that are', 11319.967, 11322.858, NULL),
  (123548, 217, 'infeasible. They would take
exponential time to solve them in', 11322.908, 11326.898, NULL),
  (123549, 217, 'the ordinary way. And they''re not
known to be polynomial time solvable,', 11326.948, 11330.878, NULL),
  (123550, 217, 'although in these cases it''s an open
question whether there is a polynomial time', 11330.948, 11334.438, NULL),
  (123551, 217, 'algorithm, a feasible algorithm. And
for most for most of the NP-complete', 11334.628, 11341.328, NULL),
  (123552, 217, 'problems, you can prove that there''s a', 11341.338, 11344.788, NULL),
  (123553, 217, 'polynomial time approximation that
solves almost all instances...', 11344.848, 11348.998, NULL),
  (123554, 217, '...in a feasible amount of time. So
like the knapsack problem, you know,', 11349.008, 11352.768, NULL),
  (123555, 217, 'packing problems, and so on, other kinds
of problems, satisfaction problems,', 11352.828, 11356.168, NULL),
  (123556, 217, 'when... Depending on how you set
up the formalism, you can prove,', 11356.248, 11360.008, NULL),
  (123557, 217, 'and I''ve proven many
instances of this but also I', 11360.048, 11363.948, NULL),
  (123558, 217, 'think it''s widespread for
almost all the NP-complete', 11363.988, 11367.708, NULL),
  (123559, 217, 'problems, the difficult problems, and these
are important problems for industrial', 11367.788, 11371.568, NULL),
  (123560, 217, 'application when these are problems that
we actually want to solve. We can have', 11371.608, 11376.708, NULL),
  (123561, 217, 'feasible algorithms that solve
almost every instance of them.', 11377.408, 11381.528, NULL),
  (123562, 217, '- The amount of fields and topics
you''ve worked on is truly', 11382.003, 11385.964, NULL),
  (123563, 217, 'incredible. I have to ask about P
versus NP. This is one of the big', 11385.983, 11389.964, NULL),
  (123564, 217, 'open problems in complexity theory. So for
people who don''t know, it''s about the relation', 11390.044, 11393.764, NULL),
  (123565, 217, 'between computation time and problem
